      "text": "To find the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) such that \\( x \\) is a positive multiple of 3 and \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we start by determining the cube roots of the numbers around one thousand. The cube root of 1000 is 10, and the third power of 10 is \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\). We are looking for the largest multiple of 3 that is less than 1000 and then cubes this multiple.\n\nLet's check the next smaller multiple of 3, which is 9. We calculate \\( 9^3 \\):\n\\[\n9^3 = 9 \\times 9 \\times 9 = 81 \\times 9 = 729\n\\]\nSince \\( 729 < 1000 \\), \\( x = 9 \\) satisfies the condition \\( x^3 < 1000 \\). We now check if there is a larger multiple of 3 that also satisfies the condition. The next multiple of 3 is 12:\n\\[\n12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 144 \\times 12 = 1728\n\\]\nSince \\( 1728 > 1000 \\), \\( x = 12 \\) does not satisfy the condition \\( x^3 < 1000 \\). Therefore, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) that is a positive multiple of 3 and satisfies \\( x^3 < 1000 \\) is 9.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
